<article class="env-exercise" id="04WY">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04WY">Exercise <span data-tag="04WY">10.2.2.16</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be the category of partially ordered sets (where morphisms are nondecreasing functions). Let $Q = \{  a, b, c, d \} $ be a set with four elements, endowed with the partial ordering indicated in the diagram </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ a \ar [r] &amp;  b &amp;  c \ar [l] \ar [r] &amp;  d. } \]
</div>
<p> Let $f: Q \rightarrow [2] = \{  0 &lt; 1 &lt; 2 \} $ be the nondecreasing function given by </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  f(a) = 0 \quad \quad f(b) = 1 = f(c) \quad \quad f(d) = 2, \]
</div>
<p> so that we have a pullback diagram of partially ordered sets </p>
<div class="equation" id="04WZ">
<span class="equation-label"><a data-tag="04WZ" href="/tag/04WZ">10.21</a></span>
<div>\begin{equation}  \begin{gathered}\label{equation:poset-not-universal} \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \{  a, d \}  \ar [r] \ar [d]^{f_0} &amp;  Q \ar [d]^{f} \\ \{  0 &lt; 2 \}  \ar [r] &amp;  [2]. } \end{gathered} \end{equation}</div>
</div>
<p> Show that $f$ is a quotient morphism in (the nerve of) the category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, but that $f_0$ is not. </p>
</article>
